WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.940
GRACE MOLLISON:
My name is Grace.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.410
I'm a Cloud solutions architect,
based out of the London office

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.380
if you were wondering where
my weird accent came from.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.239
And I'm here to talk
to you about designing

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:19.852
and implementing disaster
recovery systems in GCP

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:21.310
no matter where
your production is.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.640
So be that on GCP
itself, on-premises,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.090
or in another cloud.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.750
So when it comes
to the DR, there

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.170
tends to be a number of three
letter acronyms or TLAs.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:36.390
So I just want to start to
go through some of those.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.160
I would like to start by
actually distinguishing

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.180
between disaster recovery
and high availability.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.890
Think about high availability
as being for a micro disaster.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.760
A single web server dies
in a farm of web servers,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.500
or a disk in a SAN.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.040
To think of disaster recovery
would be when you actually

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.800
have to failover
to a recovery site

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.280
because you're no longer
able to serve your production

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.200
environment out of your
production environment.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.420
So they're orthogonal,
but they are related.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.890
HA will actually drive what
your DR scenario will look like,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.930
particularly if you're running
your production on GCP.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.790
RTO and RPO-- these are
obviously the three letter

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.910
acronyms that everybody knows
and will understand when it

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:27.560
comes to our DR. Think of RTO--

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.020
the Recovery Time Objective--
being how long will

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.610
it take you to get
your application

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.460
back up and running again.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.970
Recovery Point Objective--
think about that

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.020
as how much data
are you actually

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.480
willing or able
to actually lose.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.480
Think about going back
to that last backup

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.020
you took, or the backup and
the transaction log files.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.670
So the smaller the
RTO and RPO value,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.280
it has a asymptotic impact
on your bottom line,

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.750
i.e., it's likely to
cost more, because you're

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.260
going to have to put
build in more resilience

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.170
into your environment.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:08.550
SLA and SLOs-- they're
often conflated,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.159
but they are two
separate things.

00:02:10.159 --> 00:02:14.790
The SLO is the
measurable component.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.640
So here, the
measurable component

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.760
is how many bowls of cat
food has this cat got,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.880
and obviously how often
I give them the cat food.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.330
And the SLA is the agreement.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.190
The SLOs feed into that SLA.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.300
And also the RTO and RPO
values feed into the SLOs.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.180
Now, obviously, I'm a not
great contract negotiator,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.590
because this is going to
be one really fat cat.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.470
So moving on.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.600
So I just want to kind of get
you thinking about DR patterns

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.220
before we move on
and start actually

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.970
looking at some of the
features that can help you.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.270
And I'm going to draw an
analogy with having a journey

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.430
and having a flat tire.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.900
So if you're driving along
and you have a flat tire,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.390
you have no spare, you
come to a complete halt.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:05.880
You have to phone somebody up.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.930
You have to get them
to come out to you.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.440
They have to replace the tire.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.900
That's a pretty cold
pattern, because you're not

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.770
ready for that scenario.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.180
However, if you've got a
spare, yes, you need to halt,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.060
but you've got everything
you need to actually recover

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.470
and continue on your journey.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.950
You change the tire yourself.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.690
You don't need to
wait for anybody else.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.360
Then you can carry on.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.590
And at the other
end, the hot pattern

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.220
would be the fact if you were
running on run flat tires.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.190
Run flat tires means
if it has a puncture

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.005
you can still continue moving.

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:38.212
It may be a bit slower.

00:03:38.212 --> 00:03:40.670
And obviously when you get to
the other end of your journey

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.350
you'll change it
to a proper tire.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:43.590
But you're ready.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:48.380
It doesn't really stop you, it
just makes you slow down a bit.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.860
So we talked about patterns,
talked about the TLAs.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.260
You need to do some planning.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.300
So you need to start by
defining your recovery goals.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.200
To think about this, think
about your actual application.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.330
Because your application
will actually define what

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.920
those recovery goals are.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.690
So here we've got three
different examples

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.370
of different types
of applications.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.500
Batch processing-- that doesn't
tend to be time critical.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.320
So you can do things-- and
this is a Dataproc one--

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.810
like take advantage
of preemptible VMs.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.800
A preemptible VMs are
like standard VMs,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.570
but you can get them
at a lower cost.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.880
But we will reclaim
them up to 24 hours.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.950
So with this sort of batch
processing architecture

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.740
you can have a mix of
standard and preemptibles.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.340
So if the preemptible
gets reclaimed,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.090
if you're using
checkpoints you can either

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.020
continue processing that
little bit of workload

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.140
from that point, or
you can start again.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:50.960
It depends.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.820
But the point is,
it's a fact that it's

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.280
kind of a warmish pattern,
because you can still carry on.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.440
It slows you down--
you can carry on.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.820
E-commerce has different
RTO and RPO values

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.470
throughout the application.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.200
So the actual user
journey, that's hot.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.815
It needs to be
available all the time.

00:05:09.815 --> 00:05:13.580
If it too easy, users today have
a very short attention span,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.740
and they will move on.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.114
But the email that notifies them
that they've bought something,

00:05:19.114 --> 00:05:21.280
that's not that important,
because they know they've

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.040
bought it.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.660
Yes, they'll be expecting
an email at some point,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.940
but not immediately.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.140
It's decoupled.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:28.370
It's fine.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:31.310
And the recommendation
engine, that could actually be

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:32.990
out of date by a couple days.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:36.800
Yeah, it's not great, but it's
not critical to the journey.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.490
And then the other extreme,
the encoding and delivery,

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:40.280
everything's hot.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.500
You need have to resilience
everywhere because people

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.670
want to watch their films.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.160
The encodings got to
happen for the platform.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.950
So you can see that how
looking at your application

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:52.370
will define your recovery goals.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.880
So you identify the DR
patterns that are predictable.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.536
You need to design for
end-to-end recovery, i.e.,

00:05:58.536 --> 00:06:00.410
not just backing up,
but you need to actually

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.020
be able to go back to your
production environment

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.930
when it's up and running.

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:07.910
Make your tasks specific.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:12.690
Saying run the recovery
scenario isn't good enough.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.594
You need to actually
provide instructions,

00:06:14.594 --> 00:06:16.010
because you don't
know who's going

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:17.306
to be there to do the recovery.

00:06:17.306 --> 00:06:19.180
It might be somebody
who's not that familiar.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.800
Say, log on to the
server, change directory

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.780
to the DR folder, and then
run the file helpmeplease.sh

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.160
with a command to do that.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.450
Implement control measures-- you
don't really want a disaster,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:37.590
so take advantage of the
self-healing features

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.800
within the platform, especially
if your production is on GCP.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.180
Think HA if you can.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.990
Support-- making sure
your communication lines

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.070
are sorted out.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.160
Make sure you know
who to contact in GCP.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.880
Make sure your contact
details are up to date.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.160
And make sure internally
that the contact lines

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.720
are sorted out.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.920
Verify your software licensing--

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.460
sometimes you're not
able to run your software

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.070
that you're running
in production

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.440
in a warm standby mode.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.464
You need to double-check
those licenses.

00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:14.130
So if you're using
third party packages,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.460
really make sure that you're
running it in the correct way

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.680
that it's allowed to.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.740
What happens if you
can't connect to GCP?

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.840
If your production
environment's down,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.610
how are you going
to deploy if you're

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:32.120
deploying from production
to your DR scenario?

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.306
Implement maybe more than
one data recovery path.

00:07:36.306 --> 00:07:38.180
Think about different
ways of [INAUDIBLE] how

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:42.140
you're going to connect to GCP
in the event of a disaster.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:44.500
Test your plan regularly.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.250
You can use things
like Deployment Manager

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.890
to bring up [INAUDIBLE] your
environment and bring it down.

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.549
It's something that
makes DR in the Cloud

00:07:51.549 --> 00:07:53.090
so much more
compelling, because it's

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:56.540
just so much easier to do this.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.480
Security and your DR plan--

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:01.480
you need to maintain
permission consistency

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.170
between your on-prem and GCP.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:08.370
You've set up controls
on-prem or in another cloud,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.560
and you need to mirror
that when you failover.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.050
The permissions
you've put in place

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.870
shouldn't be forgotten about.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.480
Make sure your users can
log into the DR environment.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:22.360
Don't expect them to be able
to log into the DR environment

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.060
for the first time when
you have a disaster.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:25.690
Practice.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.210
Get them to understand
what they need to do.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.010
Train them to use
the DR environment.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:31.926
Things will be different,
particularly if it's

00:08:31.926 --> 00:08:33.427
on-prem and going to GCP.

00:08:33.427 --> 00:08:35.260
If it's within GCP and
they're used to that,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.150
then obviously it's
not a big deal.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.919
But if they're used to logging
in to their on-prem stuff,

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:43.190
you need to think about how you
get them to log on elsewhere.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:46.570
Mirror your security
controls in GCP.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.660
So if you classify
your data and you've

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.677
got particular controls around
that, mirror that in GCP.

00:08:52.677 --> 00:08:54.760
So you need to understand
how to map your security

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.210
controls to using the
things like IAM in GCP.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.700
And manage your
secrets properly.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.490
Configuration files-- if you're
running the downloaded service

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:06.770
accounts keys, manage
those properly as well--

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:08.650
use encryption and
things like that.

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:14.077
So now, I'm going to
just go through and start

00:09:14.077 --> 00:09:15.910
talking to you about
the DR building blocks.

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.900
And these are features of these
platforms that lend themselves

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.055
to DR and HA.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:29.410
Stackdriver-- often the
first sign of a problem

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.930
is with your monitors and logs.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:36.080
So Stackdriver works
across AWS and GCP--

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.944
uptime monitors, alerts,
log in and error reporting.

00:09:39.944 --> 00:09:42.360
I'll come back to Cloud Storage,
we'll dig into that a bit

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.090
[? easy. ?] But it's an object
store for things that backups,

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.020
data files.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:52.160
Cloud DNS, it allows you
to programmatically change

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:53.025
DNS values.

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:54.400
So in the event
of a failover you

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.610
can get it to automatically with
an automated failover script

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:03.420
change the [INAUDIBLE]
records to point elsewhere.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:08.070
So Cloud Storage has a
number of storage classes.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.910
Nearline and Coldline
are particularly

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.560
suited for DR scenarios.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.920
Cloud load balancing--
so a very common pattern

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.460
is to have a single IP address
that fronts either a single

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.620
serving instance or
multiple instances.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:27.890
So cloud load balancing
gives you a single anycast IP

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:28.900
address.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.870
It tends to be integrated
with managed instance groups.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.550
Managed instance
groups allow you

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:40.530
to scale up and down identically
configured out instances.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:43.530
And so, therefore it's only a
single IP address, or you can

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:47.500
have it so it only runs up a--

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.265
sorry, a single
serving application

00:10:49.265 --> 00:10:50.640
is you can have
it set so it only

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.650
serves one instance at a time.

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.690
And if that instance has
a problem, replace it,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.640
but it has exactly
the same IP address.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.395
So Compute Engine,
again, as you'd expect,

00:11:04.395 --> 00:11:06.520
I'll come back to that,
because it's the workhorse.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:07.936
That and Cloud
Storage tends to be

00:11:07.936 --> 00:11:12.990
the workhorse for DR. Kubernetes
Engine, which is our managed

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.511
Kubernetes service.

00:11:14.511 --> 00:11:16.510
And you've probably heard
a lot about Kubernetes

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.000
over this event, so I'm
not going to say too much.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.670
But basically, it allows you
to have the same workload

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.080
on multiple platforms.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.940
Cloud Deployment Manager is
kind of actually a key feature.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.120
This allows you to create
repeatable, consistent

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.320
environments.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.010
So you can define your
recovery environment

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:38.010
in Deployment Manager.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:41.040
And when there's a disaster,
you can bring it up.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.160
Also, when you want to test, you
can bring it up and bring down,

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.510
because you only pay
for what you use.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.660
So test, test, test.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:52.910
So let's have a look at some
of the components of Compute

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.650
Engine.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.770
So when you start
up an instance,

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:58.750
you start from what's
called an image.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:01.920
So this image can
be fully baked.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.400
So where it's got
everything on, it

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.320
has less external dependencies.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:10.190
So maybe for your web servers,
that's kind of a good example.

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:12.070
They have fast boot times.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:16.344
The trade-off is that that
is your version control.

00:12:16.344 --> 00:12:17.760
So therefore,
you're going to have

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.380
to manage more of those images.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:27.339
At the other end, you can start
off from of the public images

00:12:27.339 --> 00:12:28.880
and then you layer
everything on top.

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:32.220
So there's more dependencies,
it has a slower boot time.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:33.690
And then there's
the middle ground

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.689
where you might have all
your applications done,

00:12:35.689 --> 00:12:39.180
and then you only actually
load up the updated files as

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.000
and when needed.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.510
Depending on what that
instance is doing,

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:47.460
depends on which of
those you'll choose.

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:50.927
So a database probably doesn't
need to be fully baked.

00:12:50.927 --> 00:12:53.135
You probably set it up and
then you just do upgrades.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.810
Persistent disk--
so persistent disks

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.660
are block storage that
are attached to instances.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.890
Two types-- standard
and regional.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.800
So standard zone, also it
will exist in one zone.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.450
Regional uses twice
as much storage

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.520
because there's
synchronous replication

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:12.940
across two zones in a region.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.680
You need to set the permissions.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:22.040
You need to grant the
permissions to attach these two

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.360
instances.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.380
You can set the auto delete
flag on the disk to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:30.410
Sorry.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:33.780
So that means when the instance
is destroyed, that disk

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:35.570
will continue to live on.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:37.940
So you can attach
another instance

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:41.940
to that disk that's there.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.804
And also, don't forget
to mount the disk

00:13:43.804 --> 00:13:44.845
from within the instance.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.210
So at this point,
you're probably

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.040
fed up with hearing
me talk for so long.

