00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.380
It's exactly the same
with just a couple

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:35.860
of different variations in
terms of workflow or executions.

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:38.260
From a diversity
standpoint with cloud,

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.140
we want to make sure that
we are selling diversity

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:45.520
and redundancy-- that
we have a diverse group

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.050
of different cloud
providers in our arsenal.

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:49.792
So we work, obviously,
closely with GCP

00:15:49.792 --> 00:15:51.750
and the other public
cloud providers out there,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:55.030
so that we allow
diversity across clouds

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:58.270
and within clouds.

00:15:58.270 --> 00:15:59.440
So how does it work?

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.050
The technology from a
very granular fundamental

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:03.430
standpoint--

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.650
these steps are what we're
going to ask you to do,

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.090
and what the executions are
on our customers end, which

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:09.930
is install an agent initially.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.349
It's an OS-based, very
lightweight kernel agent

00:16:12.349 --> 00:16:14.140
that we install on each
machine, whether it

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:18.570
be virtual, physical, in
the cloud, or on-prem.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.070
What that agent's going to do
is connect with our management

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:24.310
console, where,
within the console,

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:26.560
you're able to configure a
blueprint which essentially

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.070
defines the resources
within GCP that are required

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.990
to then spin-up those
target instances

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:36.200
and make them available
for production.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:38.590
As Grace was saying,
test, test, test.

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:40.480
One big value prop
for CloudEndure

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.491
is our ability to
test at any frequency

00:16:43.491 --> 00:16:44.740
that our customers would like.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:47.470
They can do it on a Friday
afternoon before happy hour.

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.750
They can do it weekly,
monthly, bi-annually.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:51.821
I know that's part of
most DR planning, so it's

00:16:51.821 --> 00:16:53.320
important to
understand that you can

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:58.010
test by the click of a button
whenever it's required.

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:00.100
And then, once the
testing is completed,

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:01.683
we want to do it at
the very beginning

00:17:01.683 --> 00:17:04.272
of the implementation, and then
obviously always and often.

00:17:04.272 --> 00:17:05.980
But at that point,
we're able to failover

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:07.521
and we have those
workloads available

00:17:07.521 --> 00:17:10.960
no matter what in GCP
come time for a failure

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.625
or to run these drills.

00:17:13.625 --> 00:17:16.124
So here's kind of a high level
overview of the architecture.

00:17:16.124 --> 00:17:19.111
And I think what's
important to note

00:17:19.111 --> 00:17:21.069
is-- on the left side,
what you're going to see

00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:22.829
is your source infrastructure.

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:25.359
So whether that be on-prem
data center, whether that

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:30.820
be a GCP production environment
in east, or west, or central.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.610
So you have, obviously,
your machines working here.

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:37.500
We're going to deploy these
agents on any Windows or Linux

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:41.770
workload or machine with all
of the volumes attached to it.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.410
It's going to identify itself
up to our management console,

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:45.760
which you see up top there.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.070
What that console is going to
have is obviously the blueprint

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:48.569
piece.

00:17:48.569 --> 00:17:50.830
But all it really takes
is metadata, so the size

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:53.390
and quantity of those disks.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:56.590
What we're then going to
do through that management

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.110
console, is CloudEndure is
going to manage and orchestrate

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.230
our staging area--
the middle piece.

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.220
And essentially, all
that is is low cost, low

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.320
compute resources.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:11.320
I believe it's an n1 small or
n1 standard that we provision

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:12.916
for the replication servers.

00:18:12.916 --> 00:18:15.040
And those servers are just
recipients for the agent

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.080
traffic that then
have, I believe,

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:21.750
15 to 1 ratio from replication
server to volumes attached.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.500
And that's where you
data is going to sit.

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.710
So at this point you kind of
ignore the far right target

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:29.110
network, because this
is kind of business

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:30.986
as usual in terms of
our disaster recovery.

00:18:30.986 --> 00:18:32.860
You're going to see a
total cost of ownership

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:37.120
reduction of typically about 80%
versus legacy DR architectures.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.190
So then when we actually
need to run a test,

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:42.459
or when we need to failover
in the case of disaster,

00:18:42.459 --> 00:18:44.500
CloudEndure is going to
automatically orchestrate

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:49.130
all the necessary resources and
spin-up that target environment

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:54.200
in production with subsecond
RPOs and RTO of minutes.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:55.450
When you're done, we failback.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:57.074
We just switch the
architecture around.

00:18:57.074 --> 00:18:59.200
Source becomes target,
target becomes source.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:02.620
You can failback whether it
be deltas or full bare metal

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:05.300
and then we just turn
down that second instance

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:08.645
so you don't have to essentially
pay for it on an ongoing basis.

00:19:08.645 --> 00:19:12.040
OK, pop into here,
into the demo.

00:19:16.414 --> 00:19:17.830
So what you see
here, essentially,

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:19.000
is the management console.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:19.990
It's very simplistic.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:23.170
A lot of CloudEndure,
a lot of our approach

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:24.970
has always been simplicity.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:27.560
So to kind of dig into
this for the sake of time,

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.534
we have this list of
machines, an application

00:19:30.534 --> 00:19:32.200
server on the front-end
that essentially

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:36.100
is running a WordPress
blog, and a MySQL database

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.140
on the back-end.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:41.559
You see here on the
replication process,

00:19:41.559 --> 00:19:42.850
this is the stage that it's in.

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:44.050
So it's in CDP mode.

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:45.940
So you're having
a real-time copy

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:49.940
residing in that low
cost staging area.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:51.670
Dig into this a little bit--

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:55.184
and this is essentially that
metadata I was talking about.

00:19:55.184 --> 00:19:57.100
This is the only data
that CloudEndure is ever

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.530
going to take, or look
at, or have access to.

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:03.310
We don't ever look at the
data, have access to the data

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.510
within those workloads.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:10.091
So let's go ahead and pop
over here to our WordPress.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.030
This is not the WordPress.

00:20:19.156 --> 00:20:20.701
This is going to be interesting.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:27.600
This is the site,
but not the Press.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:32.199
So let me figure out
how to pull this up.

00:20:32.199 --> 00:20:34.740
Essentially, what we'll do is,
we'll see it in job progress--

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:37.020
I want to make sure we
don't run over time here.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.760
But this is our WordPress that
we would be typically updating.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:43.680
So I would put on there
whatever time it is--

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.740
4:56 PM, and then I
would go into here,

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:51.170
select these two instances,
and launch these machines.

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:52.920
You have two options--
tests and recovery.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:55.544
The only difference here is that
with recovery it's essentially

00:20:55.544 --> 00:20:59.160
going to prepare those instances
for a failback scenario

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:00.000
as well.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.416
So we're going to
run these tests.

00:21:01.416 --> 00:21:04.560
It's going to make
sure I want to do it.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:05.830
Here you have all your RPOs.

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:07.869
So these are the
different recovery points

00:21:07.869 --> 00:21:09.160
that you have available to you.

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:11.520
So essentially, it's every
10 minutes for an hour,

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:15.270
every hour for a day,
every day for a month.

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:18.570
And our new release will be
having customizable RPOs--

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:19.870
recovery points.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:20.864
Continue a test.

00:21:20.864 --> 00:21:23.280
So you see up top here, these
machines have been launched,

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.238
and the job progress will
provide more details.

00:21:25.238 --> 00:21:28.180
So while we're waiting
for these to launch,

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:29.869
we'll kind take a
look at this progress.

00:21:29.869 --> 00:21:31.410
The job has started
and we're waiting

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:32.451
for the latest snapshots.

00:21:32.451 --> 00:21:34.980
So right now what that's
doing is provisioning

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:37.680
all of those resources
within that GCP environment,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:41.630
it's orchestrating all the
necessary production resources,

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.880
and converting, with our
automated machine conversion,

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:48.300
any unlike source
infrastructure into something

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:49.825
that's workable in GCP.

00:21:49.825 --> 00:21:52.200
So if you have an on-prem
environment that's a little bit

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.450
legacy, like a server
2008 or something,

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:58.650
and we need to convert some of
those hypervisors or workloads

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:01.325
into something different
within the cloud within GCP,

00:22:01.325 --> 00:22:02.700
then they'll
convert that for you

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.440
all automatically
on the back-end.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.440
So let's drill down into
these machines one more

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:11.880
time and show you a little
bit about the blueprint

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.050
that I was speaking of.

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:15.090
So within these
blueprints this is

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:17.850
where we are
defining, essentially,

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.650
those resources
that are required.

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:22.670
It's just machine name,
quantity, size of the disk,

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:25.230
essentially, what we
want to run those on.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:27.270
So right here we have
an and n1 standard,

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:29.654
but, obviously, there's
different variations

00:22:29.654 --> 00:22:31.320
depending on the
complexities, the size,

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.110
and different variations
of your source workload.

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:40.690
You can customize the
name of these servers.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:43.635
So different projects or
different SQL databases,

00:22:43.635 --> 00:22:47.650
it might be 10, 15, 5,000
that you're managing.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:49.690
So in here you can
customize those.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:50.690
We define a subnet.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:52.300
So essentially it's
a private network

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:56.890
for the exclusive use of
CloudEndure in this replication

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.060
process.

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:02.770
Private IP and public IPs
as well, the disks required.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:06.460
On the network side you have the
ability to throttle bandwidth.

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:08.430
So during the initial
sync, obviously,

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:11.594
is when we're going to utilize
those resources the most.

00:23:11.594 --> 00:23:13.510
But after that, if we
wanted to just make sure

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.160
that production is never
interrupted from a bandwidth

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:17.549
perspective, you can
throttle this up and down

00:23:17.549 --> 00:23:18.340
depending on needs.

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:23.230
But typically, the
default is just fine.

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:26.290
The dashboard, essentially, is a
high level overview of licenses

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:28.780
you have allocated to
your pool of licenses,

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:30.400
what's in initial sync mode--

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:33.550
so that initial seeding of the
data into the low cost staging

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:34.390
area--

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:37.090
and what's ready, which
means it's in full CDP mode

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:41.110
and you have a
real-time copy waiting

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:43.820
for failover-- waiting for
that execution to take place.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:45.730
Typically, between
these two phases,

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:47.759
it pops into the
require attention.

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:49.175
So after the initial
sync, it will

00:23:49.175 --> 00:23:50.549
say require
attention, which just

00:23:50.549 --> 00:23:54.290
means we highly recommend that
you do some testing on this.

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:56.290
Again, we're always going
to promote test, test,

00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:59.540
test as well.

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:02.325
The audit log essentially
is an overview

00:24:02.325 --> 00:24:03.700
of all the different
transactions

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:05.100
that have taken place.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:08.470
We've run the demo a few
times the last couple of days.

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:09.720
It also shows test launch.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:11.200
You can delete the replication.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:13.210
So all of this is kind of
taking place in the back-end,

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:15.584
giving you a bit of an audit
trail for compliance reasons

00:24:15.584 --> 00:24:17.610
or for internal best practices.

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:20.770
On the setup and info,
you have the machines

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:25.755
associated with this project,
your GCP credentials.

00:24:25.755 --> 00:24:27.130
These credentials,
again, go back

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:28.960
to that kind of defining
where we're going

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:32.200
and what we're doing with it,
replication settings in terms

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:35.290
of what your source is
and what your target is.

00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:37.090
So in this case,
we have, I believe,

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:41.770
it's AWS is our demo
environment to Google Central--

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:43.639
replication servers,
hosts names, et cetera.

00:24:43.639 --> 00:24:46.180
So everything that's sitting on
that source from the OS level

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:49.480
from, the disk level, is
being replicated fully.

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:52.870
Every setting, every
security group, subnets,

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:56.690
disk sizes-- all of that
is completely, again,

00:24:56.690 --> 00:24:59.810
in real-time at block level,
replication taking place,

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.700
ready to be restored based
on that blueprint definition.

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:05.710
And some of these are
just other settings

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:08.390
that I can go into-- email
notifications and whatnot.

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:11.710
So to add a new
machine we go up here

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.030
to how to add new machines.

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:14.620
It's as simple as
deploying an agent.

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:18.820
That agent immediately syncs
with the GCP cloud environment

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:22.060
that you defined and
goes into sync mode

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:23.560
without any further
action required.

00:25:28.915 --> 00:25:31.012
Let's go back to the machines.

00:25:31.012 --> 00:25:33.470
And you'll always, again, see
which phase they're in here--

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.100
and when they were tested.

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:37.840
It looks like we actually
launched our SQL so far,

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:40.360
and our target machines
have been launched.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:47.090
So you can see that in here we
have an RTO of 7:56 PM to 8:01.

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:50.750
So about four minutes and
we have fully functional,

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:52.520
what would be a
WordPress with an update

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:58.070
to it, executed and
in failover mode.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:01.070
So from here, again,
until your source machines

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:03.500
are back up and running, we
can failback at any given time

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.300
by flipping that architecture.

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:14.180
And then the best part about
it, is we select these machines

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:17.570
and we delete these
target instances saving us

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:22.670
all the money associated with
GCP resources being consumed.

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.104
Thank you.

00:26:24.104 --> 00:26:27.056
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:29.024 --> 00:26:30.008
Thank you.

00:26:36.404 --> 00:26:38.570
GRACE MOLLISON: [INAUDIBLE]
mostly with this.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:42.070
OK, so continuing on.

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:43.990
We're going to be
tight for time.

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:48.100
So first thing that you need
to think about when connecting

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.840
to GCP is connectivity.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:53.050
You need to think about
what's going to be

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.750
passed across your bandwidth.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:59.470
You need to think about what
else is sharing that bandwidth.

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:03.520
Typically, we see one of
the flavors of Interconnect.

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:04.240
So this helps.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:06.240
This is important, because
it helps you get over

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:09.700
the variances of transferring
stuff across the internet.

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:13.430
You've got more
control over the hops.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:16.420
So once you've decided on
how you're going to connect,

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:19.960
you then need to think about
how you do the transfer.

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:22.280
Initially, you tend to
seed your environment.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:23.950
So you have a large
amount of data.

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:27.190
So use something like that
the Google Transfer Appliance

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:29.692
and then once you've got
that seeded and you've got

00:27:29.692 --> 00:27:34.740
your warm standby or whatever
on GCP, you can then use, maybe,

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:38.250
something like gsutil or
the Google Transfer Service

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:43.180
to move over
incremental updates.

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:47.160
So there's typically data
involved when you do a DR.

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:50.970
So I tend to put it into two
lots, what I call data backups

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:53.160
and database backups.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:56.760
Data backups are just
essentially a discrete amount

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:00.780
of data copied from one place
to another place, so typically

00:28:00.780 --> 00:28:02.190
Cloud Storage.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.130
Whereas database backups
tend to be more complicated,

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:09.030
because you're copying over
the backup of your database

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:12.870
using the system that your
database engine uses to create

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:15.700
the backup, and also
the transaction log

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:18.300
files, particularly for
relational databases.

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:21.060
Plus you've got to make
sure that in GCP you've

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:25.290
got the ability to run the
correct version of the database

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:27.570
server so that you
can actually restore

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:30.120
the backups and the
transaction log file.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:32.340
So it tends to be
more complicated.

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:34.809
And the key thing there is
the recovery point objective.

00:28:34.809 --> 00:28:36.600
So you've got to make
sure that you're also

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:38.391
thinking about getting
your transaction log

00:28:38.391 --> 00:28:42.340
files over a good cadence.

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:46.320
A typical pattern for DR is
a tiered storage pattern.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.900
So traditionally,
this means tiering

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:53.050
data from local fast disks
to slower, cheaper disks

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:54.490
as you move along.

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:56.510
In GCP it's slightly different.

00:28:56.510 --> 00:29:00.160
So you have the fast,
persistent disks

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:02.670
being the faster local
disks, and then you

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.860
tier it to Cloud Storage.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:10.730
So the performance is the
same for both of those.

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:16.390
And you tier, but as you
don't need access to the data

00:29:16.390 --> 00:29:19.570
as quickly, it becomes
cheaper to store

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:22.750
on Coldline or Nearline.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:24.610
And just to show you
that the patterns are

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:29.110
the same whether you
do it on-prem or not,

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.690
here we have a tiered
storage pattern,

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:33.790
whereby you've got Cloud
Interconnect connecting

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:36.450
to your on-prem to GCP.

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:40.750
You've got your
on-premise's storage array,

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:44.020
and you're tiering data
off to a regional packet,

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:46.870
and then to a Nearline
or Coldline bucket.

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:49.690
You could actually
decide that you

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:51.910
want to change the
class of the object

00:29:51.910 --> 00:29:55.810
when it gets to the
regional bucket.

00:29:55.810 --> 00:29:59.710
What I find is that most people
tend to actually just want

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:01.150
another bucket to
do the tiering,

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:03.610
because it logically
just is easier to see.

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:11.210
So GCP has a number
of managed databases.

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:15.380
And they're designed
for scale and volume.

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:19.070
So they're designed for
managing petabytes of database.

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:22.389
So laws of physics,
doing backup and restores

00:30:22.389 --> 00:30:23.930
of huge amounts of
data is just going

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:25.700
to take an awful long time.

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:28.790
So you need to think about how
you design those databases.

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:30.740
Maybe have tables
that are designed

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:32.930
so that you've
got them in dates.

00:30:32.930 --> 00:30:35.610
So if there's a corruption
for a particular date,

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:37.880
you only have you have to
worry about that sliver

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:39.980
of your database.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:43.560
These systems tend to be
replicated underneath anyway,

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:47.600
but we do understand that people
want a better level of comfort.

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:50.630
You can export things
to Cloud Storage,

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:51.980
or you could keep the raw files.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:55.700
Maybe you only need that day's
bit of data to actually get up

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:56.390
and running.

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:59.030
So you just need to
think it through.

00:30:59.030 --> 00:31:01.460
I would also like to call
out here Cloud Composer.

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:04.430
This is our manage
Apache Airflow.

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:06.520
Using a directed
acrylic graph it

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:10.070
allows you to design a flow
for moving data around.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:13.810
So you can use it, and it has
operators, which are plugins,

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:16.230
for things like BigQuery
and Cloud Storage.

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:18.500
So you can use that
to move data around

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:21.230
from BigQuery and
Cloud Storage to get

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:23.710
a backup system that way.

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:27.400
OK, fun bit, we'll look
at some architectures.

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:30.570
Let's start by looking
at production on GCP.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.820
HA first-- you need to think
about high availability when

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:36.750
you're designing your
application on GCP,

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:39.290
because they've got
these features built in--

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:41.540
so zones and regions.

00:31:41.540 --> 00:31:45.430
So we've talked about
most of these briefly.

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:48.150
So I can whiz on.

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:50.800
The security controls you don't
need to worry so much about,

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:52.810
because you're in GCP anyway.

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:55.060
So you would have defined
your security controls

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:58.020
and they would be easy to
apply to your new environment.

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:01.070
Here's an interesting
DR scenario--

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:05.290
you're running your
web service out of GCE,

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:09.730
and you've also got
a static website

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:12.880
on Cloud Storage that's sitting
there looking interesting.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:17.205
Say there's a problem with
serving your web service out

00:32:17.205 --> 00:32:18.340
of GCE.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:24.210
Then what you can do, is you
can flip Cloud DNS to point

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:25.950
to your static website.

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:28.830
If your web servers haven't
actually got that many moving

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.780
parts-- a bit JavaScript--

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:33.180
static website is
actually really

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:38.920
cost effective way of getting
something up and running.

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:42.550
You could, of course, do
HA web serving application.

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:45.484
So here we're using a
regional managed instance

00:32:45.484 --> 00:32:46.900
groups, so there's
instances cross

00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:49.090
all the zones in the region.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:52.160
If one of the zones
has a problem,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:54.300
then it will start
up an instance

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:57.640
so you could still have your
load in one of the other zones.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:00.320
At the back-end we're
using Cloud SQL,

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:02.710
and it's doing
semi-asynchronous replication

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:06.010
to the replica in
the other zone.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:09.400
When there's a problem, it
will automatically failover.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.470
Yes, your current
connections will be lost,

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:13.000
but they will be reconnected.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:15.265
But crucially, you're
still serving data.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.460
I talked about the
single IP address.

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:23.160
This is where this
example comes In so you

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:27.370
have a single running
server at any one point--

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:29.880
typically, for a database,
maybe for an application

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:30.885
middleware-type service.

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:36.970
You've taken snapshots
of this persistent disk.

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:38.860
In the event of a
problem, the snapshot

00:33:38.860 --> 00:33:41.500
creates a disk in another zone.

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:45.470
I could have used regional
persistent disks here as well.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:47.360
So that's another alternative.

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:48.790
The reason you
use snapshots here

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:55.680
is because standard
persistent disks are zonal.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:58.180
Don't get me wrong, you would
take snapshots of the regional

00:33:58.180 --> 00:33:58.730
as well.

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:01.270
But the mechanism
works different.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:03.670
I mentioned you could see
Deployment Manager on the list

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:05.530
of the building blocks here.

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:08.080
Deployment Manager is here
used to bootstrap the managed

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:13.330
instance group and also to
start up a HTTP server where

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:17.590
you are echoing your custom
metrics that Stackdriver

00:34:17.590 --> 00:34:19.180
can receive.

00:34:19.180 --> 00:34:20.679
And then that
actually kind of helps

00:34:20.679 --> 00:34:25.060
you to realize where
there's a problem

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:26.457
with the actual application.

00:34:26.457 --> 00:34:28.540
Because sometimes the
instance might be all right,

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:32.040
but it's the application
you're worried about.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:35.010
Productions on premises--
this is all about connectivity

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:37.630
really, Cloud
Storage and partners.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:39.360
We've got some
fantastic partners.

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:40.170
You've seen one.

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:41.719
We've got another
one coming up later

00:34:41.719 --> 00:34:45.746
to do another demo, because
I like people to see demos.

00:34:45.746 --> 00:34:47.620
And then there's the
security considerations.

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:50.100
This is something that you
need to really think through.

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:53.699
You need to think about
mapping your security controls

00:34:53.699 --> 00:34:56.790
and the permissions
your users have to GCP.

00:34:56.790 --> 00:34:59.340
So I spoke about that
in the planning stage.

00:34:59.340 --> 00:35:01.380
Compute Engine and
Kubernetes Engine--

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:05.350
it tends to be the Compute I
see a lot in this environment.

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:08.880
Here we have a
warm standby setup.

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:11.890
You're running production
out of your data center.

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:14.800
You'll notice that we're
actually replicating data

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:18.470
to a database on GCP.

00:35:18.470 --> 00:35:20.990
That instance is quite small.

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:27.200
It's just sized to run the
minimal to take the replicated

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:29.730
data.

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:32.930
So in the event of
a problem, Cloud DNS

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:37.190
points to web servers that
have been created by snapshots.

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:40.530
I chose snapshots here, because
if this is a fast moving

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:42.440
org in this example
and they're constantly

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:43.925
changing the web service.

00:35:43.925 --> 00:35:45.920
So snapshots were
the quickest way.

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:47.810
You could also have
chosen custom images

00:35:47.810 --> 00:35:50.250
as a way to do that.

00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:51.860
And then, obviously,
you could also,

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:54.860
to take the database to
take production workload,

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:56.720
you can enlargen
it so it can cope

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:59.630
with the production workloads.

00:35:59.630 --> 00:36:02.514
And you can have hot HA
across GCP and on-premises.

00:36:02.514 --> 00:36:04.430
If you do this, make
sure you've got something

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:07.650
that is able to work
with weighted records

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:09.905
so you can distribute
traffic across premises.

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:16.470
And at this point, I would
like to introduce Brylan from

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:20.490
Actifio-- and let's hope the
demo gods are still with us--

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:22.950
who will also talk to you
about how they can help you

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:24.370
with your DR environment.

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:25.157
Thank you.

00:36:25.157 --> 00:36:28.566
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:30.499 --> 00:36:32.040
BRYLAN ACHILLES:
