00:36:35.389 --> 00:36:37.930
So I'll start off with just a
few brief slides and then we'll

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:41.540
jump right into the demo
in the interest of time.

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:42.490
I'm from Actifio.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:44.560
Actifio is a software
company started

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:48.100
in 2009, that was created
to really modernize

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:50.380
the way customers manage data.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.390
And what our
software delivers is

00:36:52.390 --> 00:36:55.440
what our customers is refer
to as data as a service,

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:57.190
allowing customers to
access the data they

00:36:57.190 --> 00:37:00.520
need wherever they need it, for
multiple different use cases--

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:02.560
data protection, disaster
recovery, certainly,

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:04.750
but even things like test
and development, DevOps,

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:07.300
and analytics use cases.

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:11.660
Today we're going to focus
on disaster recovery in GCP.

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:13.854
So let's jump right into it.

00:37:13.854 --> 00:37:15.520
The way Actifio works,
it's a little bit

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.250
different than the previous
solution that you saw.

00:37:18.250 --> 00:37:21.610
We manage discrete
application consistent images

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:24.070
of different applications
in the environment.

00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:25.720
It might be a
virtual machine, it

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:28.630
might be an Oracle database
on a physical server,

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:31.385
it might be a SQL server,
or something like that.

00:37:31.385 --> 00:37:33.010
But all the different
workloads that we

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:36.910
manage, including things like
SAP HANA, everything we do

00:37:36.910 --> 00:37:40.510
is incremental forever updates,
moving only the changes

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:44.080
as efficiently as you possibly
can using incremental changes

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:46.840
and compression to
very efficiently move

00:37:46.840 --> 00:37:49.000
data from wherever it
is, on-prem, or maybe

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:52.880
another cloud, into its
destination like GCP.

00:37:52.880 --> 00:37:55.297
So everything we do is
discrete images at consistent,

00:37:55.297 --> 00:37:56.380
which is important for us.

00:37:56.380 --> 00:37:58.980
But it's not real-time or CDP.

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:02.140
And it's going to be
job-based and RPOs

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:06.160
along the lines of 15
minutes is what we advertise.

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:08.170
And Actifio software
runs natively in GCP.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:09.370
It runs on-premises.

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:12.520
It runs anywhere that data
is, and takes this application

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:14.770
consistent data and
provides access to it

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:17.800
wherever you'd like to use it.

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:21.220
And so for the DR scenario
into Google Cloud Platform,

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:23.570
we've really got two
different ways we can do it.

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:25.480
Actifio is all about
letting customers manage

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:28.810
their data independent of
the infrastructure involved,

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.960
whether that's on-prem or
whether that's in the cloud.

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:33.800
And so we've got
two ways to do this.

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:37.240
The first way is our
higher performance method.

00:38:37.240 --> 00:38:39.585
And that is the customer
is going to deploy Actifio.

00:38:39.585 --> 00:38:41.720
This is a DR to
the cloud scenario,

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:44.780
so the workload is on-prem
at the customer data center.

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.992
And we're going to do DR to GCP.

00:38:46.992 --> 00:38:50.880
So the customer would deploy
an Actifio virtual appliance

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:52.840
in a hypervisor of
their choice on-prem.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:55.510
We're going to capture those
changes incrementally forever.

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:58.330
We're going to provide
access to the data locally

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.882
for local recoveries.

00:38:59.882 --> 00:39:01.840
As Grace mentioned, that's
certainly important.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:04.240
You want to avoid the DR
event as long as you can.

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:07.160
If you can access
it locally, great.

00:39:07.160 --> 00:39:08.590
But obviously, to
get it into GCP,

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:10.840
we're going to replicate
that compressed, encrypted,

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:12.190
incrementally forever.

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:14.800
So it's very, very
efficient on the WAN.

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:16.270
And in GCP we're
going to send that

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:18.220
to an instance of
Actifio software.

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:21.485
In this case, our virtual
appliance, which we call Sky.

00:39:21.485 --> 00:39:24.110
It's going to be running a GCP,
it's going to receive the data,

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:26.609
and we're going to store those
application consistent images

00:39:26.609 --> 00:39:28.360
in the infrastructure
of your choice.

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:29.901
For the highest
performance, it would

00:39:29.901 --> 00:39:31.600
be Google Persistent Disk.

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:32.890
You can use a spinning disk.

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:36.670
You can use flash storage
or SSD if you like.

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:39.280
And then from there we're
going to provision the data

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:41.230
into existing virtual
machines or even create

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:43.600
a new virtual machine,
which is what we'll

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:45.940
show you in the demonstration.

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:47.890
That's the higher
performance option,

00:39:47.890 --> 00:39:50.306
which comes with a little bit
of additional infrastructure

00:39:50.306 --> 00:39:53.380
cost for that performance, for
the Google Persistent Disk that

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.900
goes with it, and the
compute running Actifio

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:57.790
software in the cloud.

00:39:57.790 --> 00:40:00.210
We have another interesting
architecture here as well,

00:40:00.210 --> 00:40:02.126
where we can do the same
thing without running

00:40:02.126 --> 00:40:03.650
any compute in the cloud.

00:40:03.650 --> 00:40:06.130
We're going to take the data
from our software on-prem

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:09.280
and just stick it directly
into object storage.

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:10.990
Object storage is
really, really cool,

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:12.940
not only for disaster
recovery, but also

00:40:12.940 --> 00:40:15.980
for data protection, which is
another use case that Actifio

00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:18.711
solves for, where we can keep
the most recent copy in object

00:40:18.711 --> 00:40:19.210
storage.

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:20.939
We can keep the copy
from 30 years ago

00:40:20.939 --> 00:40:23.230
if you want to, because it's
very, very cost effective,

00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:25.886
just by simply incrementally
updating those blocks.

00:40:25.886 --> 00:40:27.760
And you don't have to
run any compute in GCP,

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:30.970
including the Actifio software,
until the time of a disaster,

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:32.860
where you would spin-up
Actifio software,

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:34.240
point it at the object bucket--

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:36.190
the Nearline or
Coldline-- and we'd

00:40:36.190 --> 00:40:38.470
be off and running
with recoveries.

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:40.840
So it provides a slightly
slower, lower performance

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:46.300
level, but still gives you that
ability to do recoveries for DR

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:48.610
as well as backup scenarios
in the cloud, which

00:40:48.610 --> 00:40:51.370
is really cool.

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:54.360
So at this point, let's
jump to the demonstration.

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.950
If you could, please,
switch over to the laptop.

00:41:01.550 --> 00:41:02.050
Perfect.

00:41:02.050 --> 00:41:03.715
So this is our Actifio
user interface,

00:41:03.715 --> 00:41:05.840
and I can try to make up
a little bit of time here.

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:07.960
So I'll go relatively quickly.

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:10.940
This interface shows the data
that we're managing on-prem

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:11.870
and in the cloud.

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:14.871
It's based on SLAs, or
Service Level Agreements.

00:41:14.871 --> 00:41:17.120
Here you can see we're
capturing that data every eight

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:18.630
hours in this case.

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:20.060
We're keeping three
days a history

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:22.160
local for local
recoveries, and then we're

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:25.462
streaming it up into the cloud
in actually two different ways.

00:41:25.462 --> 00:41:27.920
We're keeping the most recent
version on higher performance

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:28.730
disk.

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:31.700
And in this case, we're
also doing daily versions

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:36.410
for 60 days of history in
GCP on Nearline or Coldline

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:38.436
object storage as well.

00:41:38.436 --> 00:41:39.560
So we've already done that.

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:41.268
Through the magic of
television here, I'm

00:41:41.268 --> 00:41:43.327
jumping over to
this environment,

00:41:43.327 --> 00:41:45.160
and I'm going to show
you a virtual machine.

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:50.630
This is a VMware virtual
machine on-prem called demosql1,

00:41:50.630 --> 00:41:53.666
and I'm going to click
on access my data here.

00:41:53.666 --> 00:41:55.290
There's all kinds of
things you can do,

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:57.944
but I'm keeping it
short and sweet here.

00:41:57.944 --> 00:41:59.360
I'm going to show
you the recovery

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:00.800
points that we
have in the cloud.

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:02.130
We'll take the most recent one.

00:42:02.130 --> 00:42:04.130
And I could present that
data into a running VM.

00:42:04.130 --> 00:42:05.630
If I already have a
VM running in GCP,

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:07.254
I could simply present
the data into it

00:42:07.254 --> 00:42:10.010
in a couple of minutes, which
would be that screen, which

00:42:10.010 --> 00:42:11.979
I didn't mean to click.

00:42:11.979 --> 00:42:13.770
What I wanted to do
was a different screen,

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:16.010
so apologies here--
go back to access.

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:18.350
And what I'm going to do
is recover the system.

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:19.760
We don't have the VM.

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:22.000
Let's take this
VMware VM from on-prem

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:24.170
that we've replicated into
GCP, and let's make it

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:25.852
into a GCP VM.

00:42:25.852 --> 00:42:27.060
So I'm going to come in here.

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:30.650
I'm going to call
this demosql1 in GCP.

00:42:30.650 --> 00:42:32.840
I've got some options
here, including

00:42:32.840 --> 00:42:35.690
providing authentication
with a JSON file.

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:38.330
It's got the credentials
for this particular area,

00:42:38.330 --> 00:42:42.380
and region, and zone, and
all that good stuff in GCP.

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:44.359
And I'm just going
to click the button.

00:42:44.359 --> 00:42:46.400
And what this is going to
do is essentially start

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:48.980
a job that's going to take
the data we already have,

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:51.260
orchestrate with
the APIs of GCP,

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:54.140
and create a virtual
machine from that data that

00:42:54.140 --> 00:42:57.970
was a VMware VM on-prem
just a few moments ago.

00:42:57.970 --> 00:43:00.180
So I've got a little
job monitor here,

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:05.180
which is right on this window,
and I've got a Compute Engine

00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:06.630
right here.

00:43:06.630 --> 00:43:08.630
And so, what I'm going
to do is, looking at GCP,

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.887
I'm going to filter
this for demo.

00:43:10.887 --> 00:43:12.720
And this will just take
a couple of minutes.

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:14.511
We basically provision
the virtual machine,

00:43:14.511 --> 00:43:16.610
copy the system state
information onto it,

00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:18.860
write all the Windows
registry information

00:43:18.860 --> 00:43:20.720
and boot information,
essentially

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:25.070
taking a vanilla
Windows image in GCP

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:28.180
and turning it into your
image from the on-prem virtual

00:43:28.180 --> 00:43:29.981
machine.

00:43:29.981 --> 00:43:31.480
So this will just
take a few moments

00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:35.290
to see our new virtual
machine show up.

00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:37.900
So I will impatiently
click the Refresh button,

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:39.610
as you do in a
live demonstration.

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:41.020
There it is.

00:43:41.020 --> 00:43:41.660
There we go.

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:45.035
So there's demosql1
powering on, and the process

00:43:45.035 --> 00:43:45.910
continues from there.

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:47.868
In just a few moments we
have a virtual machine

00:43:47.868 --> 00:43:51.040
running in GCP that was
on-prem in a VM or hypervisor

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:51.604
previously.

00:43:51.604 --> 00:43:53.020
If you want some
more information,

00:43:53.020 --> 00:43:54.820
we're at the booth
downstairs on the first floor

00:43:54.820 --> 00:43:55.528
of this building.

00:43:55.528 --> 00:43:58.886
So I will hand it back to
Grace, and thank you very much.

00:43:58.886 --> 00:44:00.168
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:00.168 --> 00:44:01.251
GRACE MOLLISON: Thank you.

00:44:06.460 --> 00:44:08.970
So in the interest
of time I'll whiz on.

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:11.140
So thank you very much, again.

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:13.000
Production on
another cloud-- this

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:14.950
is all about common tooling.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:17.960
So Kubernetes, and I'm
not going to add to that.

00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:20.860
There's some great, great
talks about Kubernetes that

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:22.270
can do more justice than I can.

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:25.750
But you know you can run it
on-prem, on another cloud,

00:44:25.750 --> 00:44:27.340
and on GCP--

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:28.840
various managed services.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:31.330
Cloud Storage and Amazon
S3 compatibility--

00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:34.090
you can use Boto, which
is a Python tooling that

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:38.470
allows you to write objects
to both Cloud Storage and S3

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:39.630
with the same tooling.

00:44:39.630 --> 00:44:41.620
Again, there's
connectivity, and you

00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:43.360
need to be able to
map the security

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:46.470
controls from one to the other.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:49.550
So just to wrap up,
a few tips for when

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:53.060
you're doing DR.
Identify your RTO

00:44:53.060 --> 00:44:56.930
values per system and
within the system.

00:44:56.930 --> 00:44:58.370
Identify your dependencies.

00:44:58.370 --> 00:45:01.970
They may not always be obvious.

00:45:01.970 --> 00:45:04.430
Understand the repeatable
architecture patterns--

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:07.010
so like when I talk
about tiered storage,

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:09.280
think about how
you can apply them.

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:13.040
I find it helps to list out
security considerations,

00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:17.600
permissions, as well as the
controls you need to apply.

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:24.140
And define how you will deploy
to your DR environment on GCP--

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:27.260
i.e., remember, if your
production environment isn't

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:30.050
available, how are you
going to get updates

00:45:30.050 --> 00:45:32.960
to your DR environment that
may be running in production

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:34.800
for longer than you expect.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:36.530
And test your plans regularly.

00:45:36.530 --> 00:45:37.580
You can now.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:39.470
You only pay for what you use.

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:42.290
You can use Deployment Manager
to treat your infrastructure

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:43.840
as code.

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:44.800
So do that.

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:46.640
And GCP for DR--

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:47.510
why?

00:45:47.510 --> 00:45:50.820
Total cost of ownership, you
don't have to invest upfront

00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:52.550
in mostly idle infrastructure.

00:45:52.550 --> 00:45:54.860
It's easy to carry
out fire drills--

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:55.700
test, test, test.

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:58.577
I know I keep saying
it, but really do it.

00:45:58.577 --> 00:46:00.410
And no matter where
your production is held,

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:02.036
the same approaches apply.

00:46:02.036 --> 00:46:03.410
Obviously, there
are differences,

00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:05.330
but it is the same approaches.

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:07.220
And once you start
using GCP for DR,

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:10.810
it does actually make
the migration path easy.

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:12.340
So thank you for listening.

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:15.690
